Yes, and to be honest, what other option is there? In a game that
relies so heavily on random numbers there will always be the
possibility (or, if you prefer, statistical inevitability, given a
large enough number of games played ) that the bad guy will roll all
double sixes in a row and the player will roll all double ones. It may
feel unfair to get eaten to death by a newt that the RNG decided to
make invincible, but that's not going to happen very often. On
balance, staticstically, it's fair, provided the game designer has put
the right ranges of possibilities into the RNG. The alternative is to
take away much or all of the game's randomness and uncertainty, which
would make the game all but unlosable to any half-competent, semi-
spoiled player- you might as well play one of those "choose your own
adventure" books.

A game leaning too far towards either extreme will quickly become
boring, being either too easy or arbitrarily harsh. Or, to look at it
from the other end of the telescope, optimal play becomes either
totally infallible or utterly impossible. In an open-source game
governed by a RNG, there is very little wiggle-room in between these
two extremes. At the moment, you could argue that nethack is leaning
towards the "optimal play is infallible" side, given the fact that
some people (although we should never lose sight of the fact that
"some" in this case is a very very small subset of the general
playerhood) can ascend pretty much consistently. However, you could
also argue that the RNG is actually pretty well able to deal with the
vast majority of players, and therefore the game leans toward "optimal
play can, and will, still get you killed."

Anyway, what I'm getting around too is that trying to make the game
that both (a) rewards skill and experience with eventual ascension
*and* (b) provides a challenge for an optimal player, is more or less
impossible. (All this ignoring the poor old average player, of course,
who finds the game quite hard enough as it is) Trying to get there by
balancing all the god-only-knows how many constants and probabilites
in the nethack source to get it *just right* is quite insanely
difficult. So you have to find other ways of balancing the game. You
have to find something that both the serial demigods and the
perpetually dead have in common. What do they have in common? They are
all human. All prone to make mistakes. The game designer can use that:
Encourage the player to make mistakes. Give them the chance to fix or
compound those mistakes, and then punish or reward them appropriately.
Give the player enough opportunities to do the wrong thing, and sooner
or later they will, through sheer statistics if nothing else.

This is what nethack does now, and how it should continue.

